TI  - Artificial Intelligence - Large Language Models (LLMs) and Agentic AI in Clinical Decision Support in Outpatient, General Practice and Acute Settings: A Systematic Review of Diagnostic Accuracy, Comparative Efficacy, Clinical Outcomes and Implementation Factors
AB  - Abstract Background : Large language models (LLMs) and agentic artificial intelligence (AI) systems represent a notable shift in clinical decision support systems (CDSS), offering potential to enhance diagnostic accuracy, triage efficiency, and clinical decision-making, in outpatient, general practice as well as acute care settings. Despite rapid technological advancement and increasing deployment, systematic evidence synthesis on their diagnostic accuracy, comparative efficacy, clinical outcomes, and implementation factors remains limited. This systematic review aimed to comprehensively evaluate the current evidence on LLM-based CDSS in general practice or outpatient, as well as acute and emergency settings. Methods: We conducted a systematic review following PRISMA 2020 guidelines. We searched 11 databases (PubMed/MEDLINE, Science Direct, Web of Science, IEEE Xplore, ACM, Computer Science Journals, Google Scholar, CINAHL, Academic Search Ultimate, APA PsychInfo, and Elicit AI) from January 2021 to December 2026. Inclusion criteria encompassed studies evaluating generative AI/LLMs and agentic AI in CDSS for general practice, outpatient, acute, or emergency settings, reporting original data on at least one primary outcome domain. Two independent reviewers conducted title/abstract screening and full-text eligibility assessment. Quality was assessed using the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool (MMAT). Data were extracted using a standardized framework and synthesized thematically across four domains: diagnostic accuracy, comparative efficacy, clinical outcomes, and implementation factors. Results: From 2,402 identified records, 50 studies met all inclusion criteria following deduplication and two-stage screening (inter-rater κ = 0.78-0.85). Studies were published between 2021 and 2026, with 68% published in 2025 alone, reflecting the rapid emergence of advanced LLMs in clinical evaluations. Studies evaluated LLMs including GPT-4/4o, GPT-3.5, Claude 3 (Opus/Sonnet/Haiku), Gemini 1.5 Pro/2.5 Flash, DeepSeek-R1, Llama 3, Med-PaLM 2, Doubao, Qwen, and Mistral, across emergency care (n=18, 36%), primary/general care (n=22, 44%), virtual and simulated environments (n=5, 10%), and mixed settings (n=5, 10%). Study designs included quantitative descriptive studies, encompassing benchmarks and vignettes, (n=28, 56%), retrospective observational studies (n=5, 10%), prospective observational studies (n=4, 8%), randomised controlled trials (n=4, 8%), qualitative studies (n=3, 6%), cross-sectional studies (n=5, 10%), and mixed-methods (n=1, 2%). LLMs demonstrated high diagnostic accuracy in structured vignettes and controlled settings, with advanced models (o1, GPT-4o, Claude 3.5 Sonnet) achieving ≥90% accuracy in final diagnoses in emergency internal medicine and matching specialist-level performance in cardiovascular emergencies. In the largest prospective triage study to date, screening 6,657 patients across four emergency departments, ChatGPT-4o outperformed triage staff overall (Cohen's κ = 0.833 vs 0.782; F1 = 0.897 vs 0.863), though performance declined for complex, high-acuity presentations requiring yellow and red zone triage (AUC = 0.75-0.78). Beyond struggle with complex contextual reasoning, LLMs exhibited clinically important weaknesses across atypical presentations, autonomous safety planning in psychiatric triage, and real-world clinical recommendation tasks. For example, in a study evaluating accuracy in real, unstructured clinical environment, spanning 10,000 ED visits, GPT-4 accuracy fell 8-24% below resident physicians on clinical recommendation tasks. Human-AI collaborative models consistently outperformed both independent AI and unassisted clinicians. A large vignette study generating 40,762 diagnoses across 2,133 cases demonstrated that clinician-LLM collectives outperformed all individual groups, driven by complementary error profiles. A position augmented by a real-world deployment study across 15 Kenyan primary care clinics (n = 39,849 visits), where LLM-based decision support reduced diagnostic errors by 16% and treatment errors by 13%. Similarly, Retrieval-augmented Generation (RAG) significantly improved guideline concordance (95.5% vs 62-74% for junior clinicians) and prescribing error detection by 1.5-fold over unaided pharmacists across 16 clinical specialties. Implementation findings identified automation bias (15-35% of cases), trust calibration deficits, workflow integration challenges, and regulatory uncertainty as critical barriers, with explainable AI and human-in-the-loop designs as key facilitators. Conclusions: LLMs and agentic AI CDSS show substantial promise as adjunctive co-pilot tools capable of improving diagnostic accuracy and reducing clinical errors across varied acuity and resource settings. However, significant vulnerabilities in contextual reasoning, autonomous safety planning, and unaided real world performance currently preclude their use as autonomous decision-makers. Future integration must prioritise retrieval-augmented generation, clinician oversight, explainability, and rigorous real-world prospective evaluation with patient-centred outcomes to realise safe and effective deployment.
